      "side": 1,
      "message": "This pattern is repeated in several places. Let\u0027s create a helper function that does it so we don\u0027t have to repeat it:\n\n----\n\nThis exact pattern appears four times (`IapSendCommand`, `IapRecvStatus`, `IapSendData`, `IapRecvData`), extracting it will eliminate a lot of boilerplate and centralize the casting.\n\nSince we are using C++23, you can take advantage of **`std::source_location`**. This is the modern, type-safe replacement for the `__func__` macro. It allows the helper function to automatically log the name of the *calling* function, preserving your logging strategy.\n\nHere is how you can implement this cleanly:\n\n### 1. The Helper Function\n\nAdd this private method to `IapControl`:\n\n```cpp\n#include \u003csource_location\u003e\n\nstd::expected\u003cvoid, IapError\u003e IapControl::DoUsbTransfer(\n    uint8_t endpoint, uint8_t* data, int length,\n    const std::source_location location \u003d std::source_location::current()) {\n    \n  int transferred \u003d 0;\n  int err_code \u003d libusb_interrupt_transfer(\n      dev_handle_ptr_.get(), endpoint, data, length, \u0026transferred, kTimeoutTransferMs);\n\n  if (err_code !\u003d 0) {\n    LogErr(\"[IAP] {} - fail, error code: {} ({})\\n\", \n           location.function_name(), err_code, libusb_error_name(err_code));\n    return std::unexpected(IapError::TransferFailed);\n  }\n\n  if (transferred !\u003d length) {\n    LogErr(\"[IAP] {} - fail, data transferred {} is less than expected\\n\",\n           location.function_name(), transferred);\n    return std::unexpected(IapError::TransferSizeMismatch);\n  }\n\n  return {};\n}\n\n```\n\n### 2. Updating the Callers\n\nNow, your four endpoint functions become beautifully concise. The helper handles the C-style casts and the error boilerplate, leaving the callers to focus strictly on their inputs and endpoint configuration.\n\n**For OUT endpoints (Sending data):**\n\n```cpp\nstd::expected\u003cvoid, IapError\u003e IapControl::IapSendCommand(\n    std::span\u003cconst uint8_t\u003e buff) {\n  if (!dev_handle_ptr_) return std::unexpected(IapError::InvalidDeviceHandle);\n  if (buff.empty()) return std::unexpected(IapError::BufferEmpty);\n\n  return DoUsbTransfer(kEp1Addr, const_cast\u003cuint8_t*\u003e(buff.data()), \n                       static_cast\u003cint\u003e(buff.size()));\n}\n\n```\n\n**For IN endpoints (Receiving data):**\n\n```cpp\nstd::expected\u003cvoid, IapError\u003e IapControl::IapRecvStatus(\n    std::span\u003cuint8_t\u003e buff) {\n  if (!dev_handle_ptr_) return std::unexpected(IapError::InvalidDeviceHandle);\n  if (buff.empty()) return std::unexpected(IapError::BufferEmpty);\n\n  return DoUsbTransfer(kEp2Addr, buff.data(), static_cast\u003cint\u003e(buff.size()));\n}\n\n```\n\n### Why this is a major improvement:\n\n1. **DRY (Don\u0027t Repeat Yourself):** You instantly drop about 40 lines of repetitive error-handling logic.\n2. **Log Accuracy:** Because `std::source_location::current()` is evaluated at the call site as a default argument, `location.function_name()` will still correctly print `IapSendCommand` or `IapRecvStatus` in your console logs, not `DoUsbTransfer`.\n3. **Isolates the `libusb` quirk:** The necessary but ugly `static_cast\u003cint\u003e` and `\u0026transferred` pointer logic are strictly quarantined to one place.",
