      "writtenOn": "2026-05-22T16:55:09Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "In a few places (like `IapControl::ReleaseResource` and `IapSendCommand`), there are checks against the size of containers:\n\n```c\nif (buff.size() \u003d\u003d 0) return std::unexpected(IapError::BufferEmpty);\nif (hid_interfaces_.size() \u003d\u003d 0) return {};\n\n```\n\nIdiomatic C++ heavily prefers `.empty()`:\n\n```c\nif (buff.empty()) return std::unexpected(IapError::BufferEmpty);\nif (hid_interfaces_.empty()) return {};\n\n```\n\n*Why it\u0027s better:* It reads more like English, and for some complex container types (like `std::forward_list`), `size()` is $O(N)$ whereas `empty()` is always `O(1)`. While `std::span` and `std::vector` sizes are `O(1)`, using `empty()` is a good universal habit.",
